<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ruleset name="nbh-zf1">
  <description>
    PHPCompatibility ruleset for the ZF1 port.

    The compat target is PHP 8.1-8.5, not "oldest PHP we can imagine", which is
    the part that needs configuring: PHPCompatibility is deliberately
    conservative and cannot know which interfaces in *this* codebase extend
    Serializable, nor whether a redundant-looking implementation is deliberate.

    Both answers are "yes, and deliberately" here:

      - Zend_Http_UserAgent_Device extends Serializable, so every device class
        implements it transitively and the sniff has to be told to follow it.
      - The Serializable implementations stay. Dropping the interface would
        make a no-longer-serializing object silently serialize as an empty
        O: payload instead of failing loudly, and it is published API besides.
        __serialize()/__unserialize() are added *alongside* it so the engine
        stops emitting the deprecation and stops routing through the
        blocklisted-unserialize path.

    tools/port-status.sh passes --standard=PHPCompatibility explicitly, so this
    file is not picked up automatically; pass --standard=phpcs.xml.dist to use
    it. The ledger counts therefore stay comparable across batches.
  </description>

  <rule ref="PHPCompatibility"/>

  <config name="testVersion" value="8.1-8.5"/>

  <rule ref="PHPCompatibility.Interfaces.RemovedSerializable">
    <properties>
      <property name="serializableInterfaces" type="array">
        <element value="Zend_Http_UserAgent_Device"/>
      </property>
    </properties>
  </rule>

  <rule ref="PHPCompatibility.Interfaces.RemovedSerializable.RedundantSerializableImplementation">
    <severity>0</severity>
  </rule>
</ruleset>
